the principles of design are how we use  the elements of design  they are rules that help to create an  effective composition that clearly  delivers a message to the audience  the eight principles of design are  emphasis  contrast  balance  repetition  pattern  movement  rhythm  and unity and variety  the use of these basic principles can  help a design piece stand out  without them there's a good chance that  the design will be ineffective  the first principle of design is  emphasis  emphasis is when an area of the  composition is visually dominant and  catches the viewer's attention placement  of a dominant element is critical to  design without emphasis the arrangement  of elements will not be as interesting  as it could be and the eye will be  confused  emphasis can often be achieved through  contrast  contrast is the juxtaposition of  opposing elements of design and a  composition  this could be elements like  complementary colors  different directions  tonal differences  and textural differences  contrast creates space and difference  between elements and design contrasting  elements demand the viewer's attention  and allow us to highlight key elements  contrast can make a design pop  balance is the distribution of the  visual weight of the composition weight  can come from color  shapes  size or texture  the arrangement of elements does not  allow a singular element to overpower  another  balance provides stability and structure  to a design and makes it feel stable  symmetrical balance means that elements  mirror one another on either side of a  center line  asymmetrical balance means the  composition sides are different but  still look balanced and have equilibrium  radial balance means that the elements  are arranged around a central point  and may be similar  repetition of elements creates unity  within the composition  by tying together individual elements a  composition is strengthened  repetition helps to create consistency  it can also create pattern  repetition without variation becomes  pattern  repetition works with pattern to make  the design seem active  and pattern is the uniform repetition of  any of the elements of design  [Music]  movement is the path the viewer's eye  takes through the composition  the eye is led to move from one focal  point to the next  movement can be created by lines  space  repetition shapes  color  and more  rhythm is a feeling of organized  movement  it is created when one or more elements  of design are repeated  rhythm creates mood and gives the  impression of activity  unlike pattern which demands consistency  rhythm relies on variety  variety is the use of multiple elements  of design to keep the viewers attention  and unity is the feeling of harmony  between all of the elements  too much unity creates monotony  and too much variety creates chaos  but both are needed to create areas of  interest in a composition  and that's the eight principles of  design  the more you study design the more  varied the list becomes  some add to the list and some subtract  from it  but a design doesn't have to strictly  follow these principles to be considered  good  rules are made to be broken and there  are many amazing designs out there that  ignore one or more of the principles  the principles of design are  interchangeable blocks which combine to  tell a story  understand and practice these principles  after that you can break the rules and  create your own style
 

